latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialize the output grid:\n   - Create a new grid with the same dimensions as the input grid.\n   - Copy all non-8 values from the input grid to the output grid.\n\n2. Identify and process 8-regions:\n   - Iterate through each cell in the grid.\n   - When an 8 is encountered, determine if it's:\n     a) An isolated 8\n     b) Part of a larger 8-region\n\n3. Process isolated 8s:\n   - If an 8 is surrounded by non-8 values (including grid edges), replace it with 2.\n\n4. Process larger 8-regions:\n   - For each 8 that's part of a larger region:\n     a) Determine if it's an edge cell:\n        - Check if any of its four adjacent cells (up, down, left, right) is non-8 or outside the grid.\n        - If so, replace it with 5.\n     b) If it's not an edge cell (interior):\n        - Apply the checkerboard pattern:\n          - Use the formula: 5 if (row + column) % 2 == 0 else 2\n          - This ensures the top-left of each region starts with 5.\n\n5. Ensure connectivity:\n   - The checkerboard pattern naturally maintains connectivity within regions.\n   - Edge cells (all 5) ensure connectivity between regions.\n\n6. Create and return the new ColoredGrid:\n   - After processing all cells, create a new ColoredGrid object with the processed values.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use helper functions to determine:\n  - If a cell is an edge of an 8-region\n  - If a cell is an isolated 8\n  - The correct checkerboard color for a given position\n\n- Use a single pass through the grid to apply all rules, making the algorithm efficient.\n\n- Ensure that the grid boundaries are respected when checking neighboring cells.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects of the problem:\n- Preserving non-8 colors\n- Correctly handling isolated 8s\n- Applying the edge and checkerboard rules to larger 8-regions\n- Maintaining connectivity within and between regions\n\nBy following this plan, the solution should correctly transform the input grid according to the specified rules for all given test cases and potential new cases that follow the same pattern."
